      {
       "type": "thinking",
       "thinking": "",
       "signature": ""
      },
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "Here's a practical order of steps:\n\n**1. Check the tracking**\n- Look at the latest status and location. Delays from weather, high volume, or a missed scan are common and often resolve within a day or two.\n- Confirm the delivery address on the order is correct.\n\n**2. Look for a nearby delivery**\n- Check your porch, mailbox, side door, and with neighbors.\n- Ask others in your household or building. Packages are sometimes left with the front desk or office.\n- Look for a delivery notice or photo confirmation.\n\n**3. Wait a short window**\n- If tracking says \"delivered\" but it isn't there, wait 24-48 hours. It's sometimes marked early.\n- If it's just running behind, give it until the estimated delivery window passes by a day or two.\n\n**4. Contact the seller**\n- Reach out through the store or marketplace (Amazon, Etsy, eBay, etc.).\n- Sellers are usually responsible for getting the package to you, and they can open a trace with the carrier, send a replacement, or refund you.\n\n**5. Contact the carrier**\n- Call or use the website for USPS, UPS, FedEx, DHL, or whichever carrier is handling it.\n- Ask for a trace or search, and file a missing mail request if it's been several days with no movement.\n\n**6. Escalate if needed**\n- **Marketplace protection:** Amazon, eBay, and PayPal have guarantees if an item doesn't arrive within a set time. File a claim before the deadline.\n- **Credit card dispute:** If the seller is unresponsive, you can dispute the charge with your card issuer.\n- **Insurance:** If you paid for shipping insurance, file a claim with the carrier or seller.\n\n**Helpful details to have ready:** order number, tracking number, delivery address, and the date it was expected.\n\nIf you tell me the carrier, the store, and what the tracking says, I can give more specific advice."
      }
     ],
